DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       _/_e_t_c_/_X_1_1_/_X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n is a Bourne shell (sshh(1)) script which is run when an
       X Window System session is begun by ssttaarrttxx(1) or a display manager such
       as  xxddmm(1).   (Some display managers only invoke _X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n when specifi‐
       cally directed to so by the user; see the documentation for  your  dis‐
       play  manager  to  find  out more.)  Administrators unfamiliar with the
       Bourne shell will likely  find  the  XXsseessssiioonn..ooppttiioonnss(5)  configuration
       file easier to deal with than _X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n itself.

       _X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n  is not intended to be invoked directly by the user; to be ef‐
       fective it needs to run in a  special  environment  associated  with  X
       server  initialization.   ssttaarrttxx, xxddmm, xxiinniitt(1), and other similar pro‐
       grams handle this.

       By default on a Debian system, _X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n is used by both common  methods
       of starting the X Window System, xxddmm (or another X display manager) and
       ssttaarrttxx.  To change this for xxddmm,, edit the ‘DisplayManager*session’  re‐
       source  in  the  _/_e_t_c_/_X_1_1_/_x_d_m_/_x_d_m_-_c_o_n_f_i_g  file — for other display man‐
       agers, consult their documentation.  To stop ssttaarrttxx from using _X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n
       by default, replace the contents of the _/_e_t_c_/_X_1_1_/_x_i_n_i_t_/_x_i_n_i_t_r_c file.

       The  _X_s_e_s_s_i_o_n  script is quite flexible, and extensive customization of
       the X startup procedure is possible without modifying  the  script  it‐
       self.  See “CUSTOMIZING THE STARTUP PROCEDURE” below.

